[Arch:map:The memory map]
Knight Lore is loaded, not unpacked: a snapshot taken after loading is the
whole game. It occupies from #R$6108 to the top of memory, and its variables
sit below that from $5BA0 up, with the stack below them again. Nothing lives
under $5BA0 that the game cares about.

[Arch:map2:What is where]
$5BA0-$5C07 are the game's variables and $5C08-$6107 the object table -- forty
records of thirty-two bytes. $6108-$D8F2 is the game itself, 30699 bytes of
code and data, opening with the status font at #R$6108. $D8F3-$F0F2 is the
screen buffer the drawing code composes into, and $F100 upwards holds lookup
tables the game builds for itself at start-up.

[Arch:sysvars:Living on top of the system variables]
The object table starts at $5C08, which on a stock Spectrum is LAST-K -- where
the ROM leaves the last key pressed. That is not an accident and not a
conflict: Knight Lore never calls the ROM once it is running, so the whole
system-variable area from $5BA0 up is just memory. It reads the keyboard
itself, at #R$B5F7: an IN from port $FE with the half-rows wanted on the
high byte of the address.

[Arch:loop:The main loop]
One frame is #R$AFBD walking all forty object records, followed by #R$B000
drawing the result. Each object is dispatched on its type byte through the
table at #R$B096, and the loop pushes its own continuation before jumping so
that a handler ends with a plain RET. The stack is reloaded to $5BA0 before
every object, so no handler can leave anything behind for the next one.

[Arch:random:Randomness]
There is no random number generator as such. #R$AF6C seeds from the ROM's
frame counter at start-up, and #R$AFE4 stirs in the refresh register after
every object -- so the sequence depends on how long the machine has been on
and how much work each frame did. That is what makes two games different.

[Arch:sprites:The sprites]
103 sprites share one format: a width in bytes, a height in rows, then a mask
byte and a bitmap byte for every cell. Carrying the mask with the artwork is
what lets the game draw objects in any order and still have them overlap
correctly, which is the whole trick of the isometric view. The table at
#R$7112 has a word per graphic number, and an object's first byte is that
number: walking and turning are done by changing it. The header's top two
bits are not part of the width but a record of whether the stored sprite is
currently upside down or mirrored -- the game flips sprites in place when an
object wants the other way round, and notes which way the data now lies.

[Prov:sources:What this is built from]
The code map -- which bytes are instructions, which are data, and what the
routines are called -- is derived from the Knight Lore disassembly by tcdev
(2017), as converted to SkoolKit form by Michael R. Cook (2019). Only that
factual layer is taken. None of their prose is reproduced: the commentary here
is written from the code in this build.

[Prov:licence:On reuse]
Neither of those works carries a licence, so they are treated here as
reference and credit only. Knight Lore itself is copyright 1984 Ultimate Play
the Game, and was designed and written by Tim and Chris Stamper. This
repository contains no bytes of it -- run the build against a snapshot you own
and it produces the disassembly locally.

[Prov:trust:Why the map can be trusted]
It is not taken on faith. Every byte the map describes is disassembled and
reassembled on each build and compared with the snapshot, so a boundary in the
wrong place shows up as a mismatch rather than as quiet nonsense. The build
also checks that every label and every hand-written comment lands on an
instruction boundary, because SkoolKit will silently resume decoding from the
middle of an instruction if one does not.

[Bug:moveable:The moveable block cannot be moved]
Type $3E, the block type the game builds as moveable (#R$6C27), is pushable in
its flags like the table and the chest: when the player walks into it, the
collision code (#R$CB9A) copies his velocity into the block's. But the block's
handler, #R$C4AA, clears that velocity before it moves -- and the player is
object 0, so his push always comes earlier in the same frame. The push is
cancelled every time, and the block can only ever fall.
The table's handler does the same two things the other way round: it moves by
its velocity, then clears it (#R$C4C3), so a table steps along once for every
frame it is pushed. The chest's keeps its velocity, and slides on.
Watched in the emulator: a table placed in the player's path in room $8E was
pushed 28 units in 20 frames; the same table with its type changed to $3E did
not move at all, and the player stopped against it. See the pokes for a block
that moves.

[Bug:livesdigits:Ten lives would print as a letter]
The lives at $5BBA are counted in binary -- #R$D12A takes one with DEC, and the
extra-life charm (#R$C1AB) adds one with INC -- but #R$BCA3 prints the byte as
two BCD digits through #R$BCAE. Ten lives would come out as 0 and the font's
character 10, which is the letter A.
It cannot happen in a normal game: the first life is taken as play starts,
leaving four, and there are only four extra-life charms in the castle (see
#R$6FF2), so eight is the most there can ever be. With the infinite lives poke
it cannot happen either, since the count never goes up from the start.

[Bug:chain:The depth sort's chain has no length check]
When #R$CEBB finds that an object must be drawn before the one it is
considering, it switches to it, and records the one it left in
#R$D01A so that it can tell when the order has gone round in a circle. That
list has room for seven entries, and nothing checks how many it holds: an
eighth switch in one search would write its end marker over the first byte of
#R$D022, the routine that reads the controls.
No room is known to need eight: it would take eight objects each overlapping
and in front of the last. So it is a fault the castle's rooms never bring out,
as far as has been looked.

[Poke:lives:Infinite lives]
#R$D12A takes a life with DEC (HL) at $D13F; make it a NOP. The lives shown
stay at five from the start, and dying costs nothing. Tested: a death took the
lives from 4 to 3 without the poke, and left them at 4 with it.
POKE 53567,0

[Poke:time:Endless time]
#R$C419 ends the game when the day count reaches $40, forty days. Make the
comparison $FF, which a BCD count never reaches: the days still pass and are
shown, but they never run out. Tested: with the day count set to $39 and run
on to the next dawn, the game ended without the poke and carried on with it.
POKE 50210,255

[Poke:immunity:Immunity]
#R$C82B checks, every frame, whether anything harmful has touched the player,
and starts his death if so. Make its JR Z at $C82F an unconditional JR, and
spikes, fire, guards and ghosts pass him by. Tested: flagging the player as
touched cost a life without the poke, and nothing with it.
POKE 51247,24

[Poke:moveable:A moveable block that moves]
The block type the game calls moveable clears its velocity before it moves,
which cancels every push (see the bug). Take out that CALL, at $C4AD: pushed
blocks then keep the push, and slide on -- as the chest does -- until
something stops them. Tested: a moveable block in the player's path did not
move in 20 frames without the poke, and was pushed 47 units with it.
POKE 50349,0: POKE 50350,0: POKE 50351,0

[Fact:unusedtemplates:Two templates no room uses]
Of the 29 block types in #R$6BD1, number 1 -- a fire that stands still -- and
number 17 -- spikes raised four levels -- are named by no room in the castle;
the rooms use the moving fires and the ordinary spikes instead. Both are drawn
on the templates page.

[Fact:wrongcharm:A wrong charm is destroyed, not returned]
Anything dropped into the wizard's cauldron is taken out of the game. When a
charm lands in it, #R$C245 counts it towards the fourteen only if it is the
kind the cauldron is asking for; either way its place in #R$6FF2 is cleared
and the object disappears. There are four of each kind, so a mistake costs one
of them, not the game.

[Fact:percentage:The percentage without a divide]
The score at the end is rooms visited plus twice the charms delivered, out of
128 rooms and 14 charms: 156 points. #R$BC10 turns that into a percentage
without dividing: it adds 100/156 as a 16-bit fraction once per point and
counts the carries out of the top in BCD. A last small addition makes a
perfect game come out at 100 rather than 99.

[Fact:wantedlist:The wanted list is never reset]
The order in which the cauldron asks for the charms is a list of fourteen at
#R$C27D, two of each kind. At the start of every game #R$B544 rotates it in
place by four to seven places, from the seed -- and nothing ever puts it back.
So the first game after loading can ask in only four orders, and each game
after that rotates on from where the last one left it.

[Fact:menuseed:Time on the menu picks the start]
The seed at $5BA0 goes up by one on every pass of the menu's loop (#R$BD23),
and its bottom two bits choose both the start room -- one of four, in
#R$D1E2 -- and how far the wanted list is rotated. How long a player waits on
the menu before pressing 0 decides where the game begins.

[Fact:balls:The balls chase the werewolf and flee the man]
The bouncing balls (types $B6 and $B7) move by rewriting their own code: each
frame #R$B5FF writes JR C or JR NC into two of its jumps, depending on whether
the player's legs are Sabreman's or Sabrewulf's. So the same balls hop away
from the knight and towards the werewolf.

[Fact:flipinplace:Sprites are turned round in place]
A sprite is stored once. When an object wants it facing the other way or
upside down, #R$D865 rewrites the sprite's own bytes -- rows swapped end for
end, or each row's bytes reversed through the bit-reversal table at #R$F100 --
and toggles a bit in its header to remember which way round the data now
lies. So a snapshot catches each sprite as it was last drawn: spr_014 is
stored upside down in this one, because the last thing drawn with it was the
fourth corner of the menu's border, which #R$D296 draws flipped.

[Fact:dayslength:A day is 392 frames]
The sun or moon crosses the panel's window a pixel every eight frames, and
changes over after 49 steps: 392 frames of daylight, then 392 of night, so
the forty days are 31360 of the game's frames. How long that is in minutes
depends on the rooms: a frame waits out whatever time its drawing left over,
and a busy room's frames take longer.

[Fact:objects:Forty objects, thirty-two bytes each]
The object table is exactly $5C08 to $6108, and $6108 is the first byte of the
font. The loop at #R$AFE4 detects the end of the table by comparing IX with
the address of the font rather than with a count, so the two are the same
number by construction.

[Fact:threesizes:Every room is one of three sizes]
A room record does not carry its dimensions; it carries an index into the
three-entry table at #R$6248. The castle is large but it is built from three
box sizes.

[Fact:mirror:Facing west is facing east, reversed]
Among the tables the game builds at start-up (#R$D69E) is the bit-reversal of
every byte value, at #R$F100. Mirroring a sprite horizontally is then a table
lookup per byte, which is how the game gets a knight walking west out of the
artwork for one walking east without storing it twice.

; Every memory map names each entry after its address: SkoolKit's own
; LabelColumn puts the label first, so the memory map template is SkoolKit
; 10.1's with the Label column moved after Address, and nothing else changed.
; Each map's other settings are SkoolKit's defaults, restated.
[MemoryMap:MemoryMap]
EntryTypes=bcgstuw
LabelColumn=1
PageByteColumns=1

[MemoryMap:RoutinesMap]
EntryTypes=c
LabelColumn=1

[MemoryMap:DataMap]
EntryTypes=bw
LabelColumn=1
PageByteColumns=1

[MemoryMap:MessagesMap]
EntryTypes=t
LabelColumn=1

[MemoryMap:UnusedMap]
EntryTypes=su
LabelColumn=1
LengthColumn=1
PageByteColumns=1

[MemoryMap:GameStatusBuffer]
EntryDescriptions=1
EntryTypes=g
LabelColumn=1
LengthColumn=1

[Template:memory_map]
<div class="map-intro">{MemoryMap[Intro]}</div>
<table class="map">
<tr>
<# if({MemoryMap[PageByteColumns]}) #>
<th class="map-page">Page</th>
<th class="map-byte">Byte</th>
<# endif #>
<th>Address</th>
<# if({MemoryMap[LabelColumn]}) #>
<th>Label</th>
<# endif #>
<# if({MemoryMap[LengthColumn]}) #>
<th class="map-length">Length</th>
<# endif #>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
<# foreach($entry,entries) #>
<tr>
<# if({MemoryMap[PageByteColumns]}) #>
<td class="map-page">{$entry[page]}</td>
<td class="map-byte">{$entry[byte]}</td>
<# endif #>
<td class="map-{$entry[type]}"><span id="{$entry[anchor]}"></span><a href="{$entry[href]}">{$entry[address]}</a></td>
<# if({MemoryMap[LabelColumn]}) #>
<td class="map-label"><a href="{$entry[href]}">{$entry[label]}</a></td>
<# endif #>
<# if({MemoryMap[LengthColumn]}) #>
<td class="map-length">{$entry[length]}</td>
<# endif #>
<td class="map-{$entry[type]}-desc">
<div class="map-entry-title-1{MemoryMap[EntryDescriptions]}"><a class="map-entry-title" href="{$entry[href]}">{$entry[title]}</a></div>
<# if({MemoryMap[EntryDescriptions]}) #>
<div class="map-entry-desc">
<# foreach($paragraph,$entry[description]) #>
<div class="paragraph">
{$paragraph}
</div>
<# endfor #>
</div>
<# endif #>
</td>
</tr>
<# endfor #>
</table>
